-- SUM-TYPE-FIELDS-SPEC §5.1. The refusals inside a multi-clause group; the
-- green side is `ok/sum-fields/fallthrough.l4`.
--
-- Every program below type-checked clean before this rule and then DIED AT RUN
-- TIME, with a message about a CONSIDER the drafter never wrote (§1.1) — which
-- is exactly the complaint §3 S2 exists to answer. Worse, the same logic
-- spelled as a hand-written CONSIDER was already refused with a good message,
-- so one spelling taught and the other crashed.
--
-- Sites 2, 3 and 4 are shapes NO residual could ever have covered: a selector
-- used as a value has no base at all, a base written as some other constructor
-- is already as narrow as it gets, and a nested helper's own parameter is not a
-- column of the group. Site 1 is the shape the clause narrowing DOES cover —
-- and still refuses, because the group is genuinely partial. Sites 5 and 6 are
-- the two ways of asking for more narrowing than the clauses give.
--
-- (No IMPORT: this file's golden would otherwise carry an import-resolution
-- log, and `apply` below is all that prelude was wanted for.)

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Landlord
    Agent
    Tenant   HAS monthly_rent IS A NUMBER

-- 1. THE GROUP'S OWN COLUMN, still reachable by a constructor without the
--    field. Clause 1 consumes `Landlord`, so `Agent` — and only `Agent` — is
--    what is left to reach clause 2. The message must say so, and must talk
--    about CLAUSES, not about branches of a CONSIDER that is not in the source.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE rent Landlord IS 0
DECIDE rent s        IS s's monthly_rent

-- 2. THE SELECTOR AS A VALUE, with no base at all. There is nothing for a
--    clause to have narrowed, so being in a later clause changes nothing.
GIVEN f IS A FUNCTION FROM Actor TO NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`apply` f MEANS f Agent

GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE asValue Landlord IS 0
DECIDE asValue s        IS `apply` monthly_rent

-- 3. A BASE WRITTEN AS ANOTHER CONSTRUCTOR. `theLandlord` is a `Landlord`
--    however the surrounding clauses fell out, so the read is simply wrong
--    about which constructor it reads from.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE constructed Landlord IS 0
DECIDE constructed s        IS theLandlord's monthly_rent
    WHERE
        theLandlord MEANS Landlord

-- 4. A NESTED HELPER'S OWN PARAMETER. `x` is bound by the helper's GIVEN, not
--    by the clause group, so it stands at the whole type no matter where the
--    helper is written.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE viaHelper Landlord IS 0
DECIDE viaHelper s        IS helper s
    WHERE
        GIVEN x IS AN Actor
        helper x MEANS x's monthly_rent

-- 5. NARROWED TO THE WRONG ARM, inside a later clause. The clause narrowing
--    says `Agent` or `Tenant`; the `WHEN Agent` then says `Agent`. Being in a
--    fall-through must not silence a `CONSIDER` the drafter DID write.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE wrongArm Landlord IS 0
DECIDE wrongArm a        IS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN Agent THEN a's monthly_rent
    OTHERWISE       0

-- 6. TWO COLUMNS, AND THE EARLIER CLAUSE TESTS BOTH. `dueFrom FALSE Landlord`
--    does not match clause 1, so clause 2 runs with `a = Landlord`. Nothing is
--    consumed in the second column, and the read is refused at the whole type —
--    the same program with the first column widened to the wildcard spelling is
--    case 5 of the ok file, and is safe.
GIVEN flag IS A BOOLEAN
      s    IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE dueFrom TRUE Landlord IS 0
DECIDE dueFrom flag a        IS a's monthly_rent

-- 7. A CLAUSE THAT BINDS THE PAYLOAD REFUTABLY. `Tenant 1500` matches only the
--    `Tenant`s whose rent is 1500, so it does NOT consume the constructor and a
--    `Tenant` still reaches clause 2 — which is fine, but `Landlord` and
--    `Agent` reach it too. Case 6 of the ok file is the irrefutable spelling
--    (`Tenant r`), which does consume.
GIVEN s IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE refutable (Tenant 1500) IS 1
DECIDE refutable a             IS a's monthly_rent
